23:45:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: When you set the temperature to 65°F, the air itself might eventually reach that number in both cases. But the way that air *feels* on your skin depends on a lot more than just the thermometer reading—especially humidity and airflow. That’s why AC-on 65°F can feel crisp and cool, while AC-off 65°F can feel stuffy and warmer. Here’s what’s really going on: ### 1. Humidity: the invisible game‑changer - **AC on = dehumidifier running.** As the air conditioning cools the air, it pulls moisture out of it (condensation on the evaporator coil). Drier air makes it much easier for sweat to evaporate from your skin, which cools you down dramatically—so 65°F with low humidity feels fresh and chilly. - **AC off = just a fan.** If the AC compressor isn’t running, the system is simply blowing outside air or recirculated cabin air at you. That air might be 65°F, but if it’s humid (common in many climates, or just from passengers breathing), sweat doesn’t evaporate well. You feel clammy or warmer even though the thermometer says 65°F. It’s the same reason 65°F in a dry desert feels pleasant while 65°F in a misty rainforest feels sticky and warmer. ### 2. Air temperature vs. vent temperature With AC on, the air coming out of the vents is often significantly colder than 65°F (maybe 40–50°F) so that the system can quickly bring the cabin to the set point and keep it there while removing humidity. That cold blast on your skin creates a wind‑chill effect and feels immediately cooling. Once the cabin hits 65°F, the system might cycle or blend, but the air you feel directly from the vents is still cool and dry. With AC off, the vent air is basically the cabin’s ambient 65°F air (or outside air at that temp), so there’s no extra “chill” on your skin, and no wind‑chill bonus. ### 3. Radiant heat from sunlight Even if the cabin air is a steady 65°F, solar radiation coming through the windows heats your skin and the interior surfaces directly. An AC 